/**
@mainpage Multiply and Replenish

A vocal pitch editor. Open a take, and the melody in it is heard note by note, laid out on a
piano roll, and re-sung at whatever pitches you put the notes on. Every tab is another edit of the
same take, and they all play at once, so a second tab moved up a third is a harmony. Nothing is sent to a server and
nothing runs live: the whole take is read once and rendered offline, a couple of seconds at a
time, so that an edit is audible before you have finished making it.

@section engine The engine

multiplyandreplenish::MelSynthesiser runs **PC-NSF-HiFiGAN**, a vocoder conditioned on a mel spectrogram and a
fundamental separately. The mel carries the timbre and the words, the fundamental carries the tune,
and the network was trained on pairs where the two disagree. It therefore re-sings whoever was
recorded, with no model of them, which is what makes it a corrector rather than a converter.

multiplyandreplenish::FcpeDetector hears the melody, behind the multiplyandreplenish::PitchDetector interface a second detector
would use if one were ever added.

@section correcting What correction does

Each note is laid out on a base line — a staircase at the note's pitch, smoothed by a raised cosine
so it arrives and leaves the way a slide does rather than as a step — and multiplyandreplenish::measureDeviation
records the difference between what was actually sung and that line, once, when the take is
analysed. Moving a note only moves the line; what was sung around it is untouched.

multiplyandreplenish::composeMelody reassembles the two for playback, running each note's deviation through
multiplyandreplenish::PitchTools first: tilted, scaled for vibrato, and faded at its boundaries into whatever the
neighbouring note is doing. Nothing here is destructive — the deviation measured at analysis can
always be recomposed exactly, which is what makes an unedited note play back as the recording.

@section layout Layout

Only the plug-in's own scaffolding sits at the top of `src/`; everything else is grouped by layer,
and includes are path-qualified so a file's dependencies name their layer.

| Directory | Contents |
| --- | --- |
| `src/` | multiplyandreplenish::PluginProcessor (the take, its tabs and its projects) and multiplyandreplenish::PluginEditor, the plug-in itself |
| `src/dsp/` | multiplyandreplenish::SincResampler, multiplyandreplenish::ZeroPhaseFilter, multiplyandreplenish::MelSpectrogram, filter banks, smoothing, pitch maths |
| `src/nn/` | multiplyandreplenish::PitchDetector and multiplyandreplenish::Synthesiser and their implementations, the ONNX session, the model search |
| `src/common/` | multiplyandreplenish::EditDocument, multiplyandreplenish::segmentNotes, multiplyandreplenish::composeMelody, multiplyandreplenish::RenderScheduler, multiplyandreplenish::Pipeline, multiplyandreplenish::TransportPlayer |
| `src/ui/` | multiplyandreplenish::EditorView, multiplyandreplenish::NoteGrid, the tab bar and the toolbar |
| `src/ara/` | the ARA document controller and playback renderer, so the plug-in edits audio already on the host's timeline |

@section threads Which thread does what

The message thread owns the document and the editor and never blocks. multiplyandreplenish::Pipeline runs the
detector and reads the recording on its own thread, handing back the melody as soon as it is
heard so the editor is usable while the engine is still loading. multiplyandreplenish::RenderScheduler owns a
second thread and renders one span at a time, nearest the playhead first; the audio thread reads
finished spans through a sequence counter and falls back to the recording for anything not
rendered yet, so playback never waits.
*/
